<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.11.2: The Factored Form of a Quadratic Expression and the X-Intercepts of Its Graph</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01924</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.11.2: The Factored Form of a Quadratic Expression and the X-Intercepts of Its Graph</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d888f675-5c48-481a-bafa-48bdcdd60680</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Consider two functions defined by \(f(x)=x(x+4)\) and \(g(x)=x(x-4)\).</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>Consider the function defined by \(f(x)=x(x+4)\).</item>
      </list>
      <para>Then, answer questions a–g, and create a table of values in which to enter the given x values and their outputs using the function.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise id="fbf538bb-f2c6-4352-9d03-9245f6291f4a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>When the value of \(x=-4\), what is the value of \(f(x)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 0</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 0.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_7e3b60e8-2698-4351-b519-0dbf05268d6c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>When the value of \(x=-3\), what is the value of \(f(x)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! -3</para>
            <para>The correct answer is -3.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_36eff251-576a-4a08-a292-23ebe6b0caed" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>When the value of \(x=0\), what is the value of \(f(x)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 0</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 0.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="c7ec1749-f6a9-420d-b76f-5177b09b99d9" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>When the value of \(x=1\), what is the value of \(f(x)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 5</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 5.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_4ead2d8e-cb7a-4723-884e-3f093a5cfd92" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>When the value of \(x=2\), what is the value of \(f(x)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 12</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 12.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_3e2149b6-ffc9-4aa2-b353-5138c9cac85c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>When the value of \(x=3\), what is the value of \(f(x)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 21</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 21.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="d7ee71b0-6b6c-47ba-9a7a-331832b8b0b4" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>When the value of \(x=5\), what is the value of \(f(x)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 45</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 45.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>Now, answer questions a–d to analyze the function defined by \(f(x)=x(x+4)\).</item>
      </list>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The \(x\)-intercepts for function \(f\) are \((-4,0)\) and \((0,0)\). These points have an \(x\)-coordinate that makes the output value 0.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="cd4fd094-81f0-44ce-bdc2-7c8ffd4a284c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>What is the value of the \(x\)-coordinate in the vertex of the graph?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! -2</para>
            <para>The correct answer is -2.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="efc5a9b1-58bb-48f7-afa5-e4c45538c19e" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>What is the value of the \(y\)-coordinate in the vertex of the graph?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! -4</para>
            <para>The correct answer is -4.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What is the vertex of the graph of function \(f\)? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The vertex appears to be at \((-2,-4)\). From the tables, the function values seem to change from decreasing to increasing at those points.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>Consider the function defined by \(g(x)=x(x-4)\).
    
    Then, answer questions a–g, and create a table of values in which to enter the given x values and their outputs using the function.
 </item>
      </list>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="e2b10c58-e3be-4468-b659-41230961de8d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>When the value of \(x=-4\), what is the value of \(g(x)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 32</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 32.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_0ded0a1a-af60-483d-8d79-07df7ca15ea9" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>When the value of \(x=-3\), what is the value of \(g(x)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 21</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 21.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_22c89b68-2f83-45e6-9397-cb6cccd55df5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>When the value of \(x=0\), what is the value of \(g(x)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 0</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 0.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="fc365c44-9ee1-4f0b-b7f9-4e1165326aad" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>When the value of \(x=1\), what is the value of \(g(x)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! -3</para>
            <para>The correct answer is -3.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="dcbf8f49-f691-4ee2-b84d-5e96b1cfd5d4" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>When the value of \(x=2\), what is the value of \(g(x)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! -4</para>
            <para>The correct answer is -4.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_570ad872-751f-4c87-b55b-f71032c93d75" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>When the value of \(x=4\), what is the value of \(g(x)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 0</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 0.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_5b0b1aff-8af2-4e7b-9fdc-0c41da2f0930" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>When the value of \(x=5\), what is the value of \(g(x)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 5</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 5.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>Now, answer questions a–d to analyze the function defined by \(g(x)=x(x-4)\).</item>
      </list>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The \(x\)-intercepts for function \(g\) are \((0,0)\) and \((4,0)\). These points have an \(x\)-coordinate that makes the output value 0.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="_3192dc06-76cb-40d2-9e9b-1ead9c311094" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>What is the value of the \(x\)-coordinate in the vertex of the graph?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 2</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 2.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_94ee4a87-d64f-4f3a-ad32-31ba6177aa8b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>What is the value of the \(y\)-coordinate in the vertex of the graph?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! -4</para>
            <para>The correct answer is -4.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What is the vertex of the graph of function \(g\)? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The vertex appears to be at \((2,-4)\). From the tables, the function values seem to change from decreasing to increasing at those points.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course.</item>
      </list>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Plot the points from the tables on the same coordinate plane. (Consider using different colors or markings for each set of points so you can tell them apart.)</para>
            <para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Coordinate grid with two graphs shown by plotted points.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_13_03_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Make a couple of observations about how the two graphs compare.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <list>
            <item> The \(x\)-intercept of both graphs is at \((0,0)\).</item>
            <item> The graphs seem to be a reflection of each other across the \(y\)-axis.</item>
            <item> The vertices both have a \(y\)-coordinate of -4 and also mirror one another across the \(y\)-axis.</item>
            <item> Each function mirrors itself across a vertical line through its vertex.</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>

    <note class="self-check"><para>The table for function \(f\) is below. What is the vertex of the graph of \(f\)?</para>
      <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
        <tgroup cols="7"><tbody><row>
            <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> -3 </entry>
            <entry> -2 </entry>
            <entry> -1 </entry>
            <entry> 0 </entry>
            <entry> 1 </entry>
            <entry> 2 </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><emphasis>\(f(x)\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> 0 </entry>
            <entry> -1 </entry>
            <entry> -3 </entry>
            <entry> -1 </entry>
            <entry> 0 </entry>
            <entry> 2 </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      </note>

      <section id="sect-00001"><title>Determining Parts of a Graph from a Table</title>
        <para id="para-00001">Identify the \(x\)-intercepts and vertex of the quadratic from the table:</para>
        <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00001">
          <tgroup cols="6"><tbody><row>
              <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis></entry><entry>
                <para id="para-00002">-5</para>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <para id="para-00003">-3</para>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <para id="para-00004">-1</para>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <para id="para-00005">0</para>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <para id="para-00006">1</para>
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry><emphasis>\(f(x)\)</emphasis></entry><entry>
                <para id="para-00007">16</para>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <para id="para-00008">0</para>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <para id="para-00009">-2</para>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <para id="para-00010">0</para>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <para id="para-00011">16</para>
              </entry>
            </row>
          </tbody></tgroup></table>
        
        <para id="para-00012">The \(x\)-intercepts are where the \(y\)-values are 0.</para>
        <para id="para-00013">This occurs at \((-3,0)\) and \((0,0)\).</para>
        <para id="para-00014">The vertex occurs where the function will create a mirror image, or be symmetrical for all points. This often occurs halfway between the two zeros (when there are two zeros).</para>
        <para id="para-00015">For this function, the vertex is at \((-1,-2)\).</para>
        <title> Try It: Determining Parts of a Graph from a Table </title>
        <example id="exam-00001"><exercise>
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            
            <para id="para-00016">Determine the vertex from the table:</para>
            
            <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00002">
              <tgroup cols="7"><tbody><row>
                  <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis></entry><entry>
                    <para id="para-00017">5</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00018">6</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00019">7</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00020">8</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00021">9</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00022">10</para>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis>\(f(x)\)</emphasis></entry><entry>
                    <para id="para-00023">0</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00024">4</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00025">8</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00026">4</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00027">0</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00028">-2</para>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
            
            
          
          <para id="para-00029">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          
          </problem><solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00030">Here is how to determine the vertex from the table:</para>
          
          <para id="para-00031">The zeros are located at \((5, 0)\) and \((9, 0)\).
            The vertex will be halfway between the two zeros. This occurs at \(x = 7\).</para>
          
          <para id="para-00032">The point in the table at \(x = 7\) is \((7, 8)\).</para>
          
          <para id="para-00033">This is the vertex.</para>
        
        </solution></exercise>
        </example></section>
  </content>
